{:id "005-version-authorship"
 ;; Until here personalist recorded *nothing* about who wrote a version.
 ;; `wrap-auth` built a principal, used it to answer "may you write this
 ;; persona?", and threw it away; an identity version row carried content and a
 ;; timestamp and no author. So `specs/startup.md` could say, truthfully, that
 ;; "a persona written by a machine is indistinguishable from any other". This
 ;; column is what removes that sentence.
 ;;
 ;; ONE COLUMN, HOLDING EITHER THE LITERAL `human` OR THE MACHINE USER'S NAME.
 ;; A single string marker is what cookbook (`recipes.source`) and rhizome
 ;; (`description_source`) both do, and it is exactly the shape
 ;; `et.uvt.caution/assess` consumes — the library never asks what a marker
 ;; *means*, only whether two of them are equal, which is what lets a name go in
 ;; a column where those two apps keep a category. `et.pe.provenance/ours` is
 ;; then `#{"human"}` and every machine name falls to *them* automatically,
 ;; including a machine user created years after this migration, with no code to
 ;; update and no enumeration to keep in step. An unrecognised marker landing on
 ;; *them* is also the right direction to fail: at worst an agent is told to be
 ;; careful where it need not have been.
 :up [;; SQLite refuses NOT NULL on an added column only when there is no
      ;; default; with one it is legal, and the column is written into every
      ;; existing row as it is added. So the constraint and the retrofit are the
      ;; same statement, and one ALTER TABLE stamps every version that exists
      ;; today `human`.
      ;;
      ;; **That is recording the owner's answer, not a schema guessing one.** He
      ;; was asked and said it in as many words: *retrofit that all old versions,
      ;; everything is stamped human.* He is the one person in a position to
      ;; settle it — nothing but his own hands and the seed script had written
      ;; into this database when those versions were made — and a fact written
      ;; down by the only party who knows it is a different act from a schema
      ;; picking a plausible value. Cookbook's `010-backfill-version-source`
      ;; draws exactly this distinction, at greater length, and against the same
      ;; question.
      ;;
      ;; **But the default must not become the way authorship gets set.** A write
      ;; path that forgot to pass an author would silently claim a human wrote it
      ;; — a false claim, and in the dangerous direction: it would tell an agent
      ;; a line is the owner's when it is the agent's own. The database cannot
      ;; tell that apart from a real claim, so the loud failure lives in the
      ;; function signature instead: `author` is a **required positional
      ;; argument** of `ds/save-identity-version` and `ds/add-identity`, never an
      ;; entry in their trailing opts map. Forgetting it is then an arity error
      ;; where it is written, not a lie in a row nobody reads again.
      ;;
      ;; No table rebuild, which is the other half of why this is one statement:
      ;; `identities` keeps its `sqlite_sequence` row, so none of the
      ;; high-water-mark care 003 and 004 needed applies here. An identity id is
      ;; never reused because nothing ever destroys the counter.
      "ALTER TABLE identities ADD COLUMN author TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT 'human'"]

 ;; The column goes and nothing else does — `ALTER TABLE ... DROP COLUMN` has
 ;; been legal since SQLite 3.35 and the driver here is well past that, so this
 ;; needs no rebuild either. It destroys every attribution on the way down, and
 ;; does not pretend otherwise: re-migrating afterwards stamps the whole table
 ;; `human` again, including versions a machine user wrote in between. That is
 ;; the honest consequence of rolling back the only place the fact was kept.
 :down ["ALTER TABLE identities DROP COLUMN author"]
 :transactions false}
